# netscan configuration.
#
# netscan NEVER blocks or changes a device — it only records and alerts.
# There is no enforce flag here because there is no enforcement path.

# Networks to sweep. CT100 has one LAN interface (eth0, 192.168.1.0/24), so
# that is all it can ARP-scan today.
#
# ⚠️ VLAN 10 (the IoT network) is NOT covered, and as of
# 2026-09-21 that is the RIGHT call — do not "fix" it without re-checking.
#
# Measured: 22 IoT-class devices (WiZ, Espressif, Broadlink, Sonos, Nest) are
# all on 192.168.1.0/24 — the MAIN LAN. The bulk migration to the IoT SSID
# never happened, so VLAN10 is effectively empty and netscan already covers
# 100% of where the IoT devices actually live.
#
# Covering VLAN10 would need a trunk port on the TL-SG108E, VLAN filtering on
# vmbr0 (currently 0 on both nodes) and a second NIC on CT100. That risks
# dropping a Proxmox node off the network, and it would put a management
# container on BOTH sides of the very boundary the segmentation exists to
# enforce. Revisit only once devices are actually moved onto VLAN10.
#
# An ARP sweep is layer 2: adding a network here without an interface on it
# returns zero hosts SILENTLY, which is worse than not scanning it.
networks:
  - 192.168.1.0/24

# TCP connect scan of discovered hosts. Slower than discovery — this is why
# the port sweep runs daily and the discovery sweep runs every 15 minutes.
port_scan: true
top_ports: 200

# Hosts whose absence from a sweep is worth waking up for. Deliberately short:
# phones, laptops and the printer come and go, and alerting on that teaches
# you to ignore the channel. These are infrastructure that should always answer.
critical:
  - 192.168.1.120   # CT100 — Hermes, warden, Caddy
  - 192.168.1.10    # c1-pn2 — Proxmox node
  - 192.168.1.20    # C1-G4  — Proxmox node
  - 192.168.1.204   # CT106 — CrowdSec LAPI

alert: true

# Hours from the very first sweep during which a newly-seen device is adopted
# silently, with no NEW_DEVICE alert.
#
# ⚠️ Do not set this to 0. An ARP sweep only sees devices that are awake:
# measured on this LAN, two sweeps twelve minutes apart returned 48 then 46
# devices, and six of the "new" ones were WiZ bulbs, a Nest and the TV that had
# simply been asleep. Without a learning window those become recurring false
# alarms and the channel stops being worth reading.
learn_hours: 48
